==Participating teams==

===Qualification===
{{More citations needed section|date=August 2021}}{{main|1994 FIFA World Cup qualification}}

Four teams—one African, one Asian, and two European—made their debuts at the 1994 tournament. [[Nigeria national football team|Nigeria]] qualified from the African zone alongside [[Cameroon national football team|Cameroon]] and [[Morocco national football team|Morocco]] as [[Confederation of African Football|CAF]] was granted three spots as a result of the strong performances by African teams in [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]] and [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]]. In the Asian zone, [[Saudi Arabia national football team|Saudi Arabia]] qualified for the first time by topping the final round group ahead of [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]] as both edged out [[Japan national football team|Japan]], which was close to making its own World Cup debut, but was denied by [[Iraq national football team|Iraq]] in what became known as the "[[Agony of Doha]]".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://inside.fifa.com/news/origin1904-p.cxm.fifa.com/seven-goal-thriller-sends-saudis-to-usa-1994-814858|title=Seven-goal thriller sends Saudis to USA 1994|website=inside.fifa.com}}</ref> In the European zone, [[Greece national football team|Greece]] made its first World Cup appearance after topping a group from which [[Russia national football team|Russia]] also qualified, competing for the first time following the [[dissolution of the Soviet Union]].

The defending champion, [[Germany national football team|West Germany]], had [[German reunification|reunited]] with [[East Germany national football team|East Germany]], so a united Germany took part for the first time since the [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938 World Cup]]. [[Norway national football team|Norway]] qualified for the first time since 1938, [[Bolivia national football team|Bolivia]] for the first time since [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]] (and the last time as of 2026), and [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]] for the first time since [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/world-cup-1994-qualifying-ireland-b1723754.html|title=Feuds, tragedy and glory: Reliving one of football's wildest nights|date=November 17, 2020|website=The Independent|access-date=July 7, 2026|archive-date=July 14, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260714102456/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/world-cup-1994-qualifying-ireland-b1723754.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Norway's 56-year gap between appearances in the tournament equaled [[Egypt national football team|Egypt]]'s record from the previous tournament as the longest. [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] qualified for the first time since [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]]. [[England national football team|England]] was notably absent; after finishing in fourth place in [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]], it failed to qualify for the 1994 event, finishing third in its qualifying group.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1993/11/24/sports/IHT-will-the-bulldogs-fall-give-rise-to-a-new-breed-of-english-soccer.html|title=Will the Bulldog's Fall Give Rise to a New Breed of English Soccer?|first1=Rob|last1=Hughes|first2=International Herald|last2=Tribune|date=November 24, 1993|via=NYTimes.com|access-date=July 1, 2026|archive-date=December 2, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241202111646/https://www.nytimes.com/1993/11/24/sports/IHT-will-the-bulldogs-fall-give-rise-to-a-new-breed-of-english-soccer.html|url-status=live}}</ref> This was England's first failure to qualify for a World Cup since [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]]. It was also the first time that none of the home nations ([[England]], [[Northern Ireland]], [[Scotland]], and [[Wales]]) qualified since first entering in 1950.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.deseret.com/1993/11/18/19077190/italy-is-in-but-britain-france-shut-out-of-1994-world-cup/|title=ITALY IS IN, BUT BRITAIN, FRANCE SHUT OUT OF 1994 WORLD CUP|first=Associated|last=Press|date=November 18, 1993|website=Deseret News|access-date=July 7, 2026|archive-date=July 14, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260714010415/https://www.deseret.com/1993/11/18/19077190/italy-is-in-but-britain-france-shut-out-of-1994-world-cup/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-06-08-sp-1711-story.html|title=WORLD CUP '94: 9 Days and Counting : The Sun Has Set : The British Soccer Empire Is No More, and Its Fans Aren't Happy About It|date=June 8, 1994|website=Los Angeles Times|access-date=July 7, 2026|archive-date=July 9, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260709141241/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1994-06-08-sp-1711-story.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24962509|title=Paul Bodin on when Wales were a spot-kick away from the World Cup|date=November 17, 2013|via=www.bbc.co.uk|access-date=July 1, 2026|archive-date=July 1, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260701100020/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/24962509|url-status=live}}</ref> Following its failure to qualify for the [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]] tournament, [[France national football team|France]] again missed the 1994 edition after conceding a goal in the final minute of its concluding qualifying match against [[Bulgaria national football team|Bulgaria]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2012/nov/16/from-vault-ginola-houllier-france-feud|title=From the Vault: The cross that cost France and started a 19-year feud|first=Paul|last=Campbell|date=November 16, 2012|via=The Guardian|access-date=July 2, 2023|archive-date=November 23, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181123065620/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2012/nov/16/from-vault-ginola-houllier-france-feud|url-status=live}}</ref> The resulting defeat left France third in the group, behind both Bulgaria and [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]].

The qualification campaigns of [[Czechoslovakia national football team|Czechoslovakia]] and [[Yugoslavia national football team|Yugoslavia]] were affected by political events. The nation of [[Czechoslovakia]] [[Dissolution of Czechoslovakia|dissolved]] in 1993, completing its qualifying group under the name "Representation of Czechs and Slovaks" (RCS), but failed to qualify for the finals, having been edged out by [[Romania national football team|Romania]] and [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] in [[1994 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 4|Group 4]]. Yugoslavia (which was supposed to play in [[1994 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 5|Group 5]]) was suspended from international competition in 1992 as part of [[United Nations Security Council Resolution 757|United Nations sanctions]] against the [[Serbia and Montenegro|country]] as a result of the [[Yugoslav Wars]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1992/10/01/Yugoslavia-banned-for-1994-World/8232717912000/|title=Yugoslavia banned for 1994 World – UPI Archives|website=UPI|access-date=July 1, 2026|archive-date=November 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221113090508/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1992/10/01/Yugoslavia-banned-for-1994-World/8232717912000/|url-status=live}}</ref> The sanctions were not lifted until 1994, by which time it was no longer possible for the team to qualify. [[Chile national football team|Chile]]'s suspension from the 1990 World Cup, following [[El Maracanazo|the forced interruption]] of its qualification game against [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]], extended to the 1994 qualifiers as well.

